\ The Hobbit: the Spectrum's memory, loaded from the game's tape, and the
\ table routines everything else uses. Every table and game variable stays
\ at its original address and in its original format, so each telic word
\ stands for one routine of the 1982 program; the hex address in its
\ comment is that routine's, as documented in the annotated disassembly of
\ v1.0 by basf-c60 (https://github.com/basf-c60/thehobbitv1.0).
\ The Hobbit (1982) is by Veronika Megler and Philip Mitchell, published by
\ Melbourne House.

\ === the memory ===

\ the main block of a TZX tape as [ tape start length ]: its last standard-speed data block, without the flag and checksum bytes
: tape-main-block ( path -- found )
	| path |
	path read-file string>byte-vector to tape
	tape dim drop to size-of-tape
	10 to position
	-1 to start
	0 to length
	begin position size-of-tape < while
		tape position @e to kind
		kind 16 = if
			tape position 3 + @e tape position 4 + @e 256 * + to length
			position 5 + to start
			start length + to position
		else
			kind 48 = if position 2 + tape position 1+ @e + to position else "expected a TZX of standard-speed blocks" throw then
		then
	repeat
	start 0 < if "no data block in the tape" throw then
	[ tape start 1+ length 2 - ] ;

65536 1 0-matrix to mem

\ a byte of memory
: b@ ( address -- byte ) mem swap @e ;

\ store a byte of memory
: b! ( byte address -- )
	| byte address |
	mem byte 255 bit-and address !e drop ;

\ a 16-bit little-endian word of memory
: w@ ( address -- word ) dup b@ swap 1+ b@ 256 * + ;

\ store a 16-bit little-endian word
: w! ( word address -- )
	| word address |
	word 255 bit-and address b!
	word 8 rshift 255 bit-and address 1+ b! ;

\ test bit n of a byte of memory
: bit? ( n address -- flag ) b@ swap rshift 1 bit-and ;

\ set bit n of a byte of memory
: bit-set ( n address -- )
	| n address |
	address b@ 1 n lshift bit-or address b! ;

\ clear bit n of a byte of memory
: bit-clear ( n address -- )
	| n address |
	address b@ 1 n lshift 255 bit-xor bit-and address b! ;

\ copy count bytes, lowest address first as LDIR does
: copy-bytes ( source destination count -- )
	| source destination count |
	0 count 1 do k source k + b@ destination k + b! loop ;

\ load the tape's main block at 0x6000, and fill the rest of memory, which the random numbers read, with random bytes
: load-tape ( path -- )
	tape-main-block to found
	found 0 @i to tape
	found 1 @i to first
	found 2 @i to length
	0 65536 1 do k mem 256 random-int k !e drop loop
	0 length 1 do k mem tape first k + @e 24576 k + !e drop loop ;

